.TH CTI-AGENT-ONBOARD 1 "2026-11-25" "cti-agent" "CTI Fleet Manual"
.SH NAME
cti-agent-onboard \- set up a box: which lanes it runs, their settings, their secrets
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sudo cti\-agent\-onboard
.BR \-\-dry\-run " | " \-\-for\-real
.RB [ \-\-lanes
.IR LANE ,...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Run it after
.BR install\-fedora.sh ,
on a new box or when adding a lane. It asks three things, in order.
.PP
.B The lanes.
Which lanes this box runs, by the names in
.IR /opt/cti\-agent/share/lanes.list .
The answer becomes
.B FLEET_LANES
(left unset when every lane is chosen). Enter keeps what is set.
.PP
.B The settings.
Each setting a chosen lane's programs read that is still empty and is one to
fill in: required in
.IR settings.manifest ,
or set (not commented out) in the lane's example in
.IR /opt/cti\-agent/share/fleet.d.example/ .
The example's comment is shown first; Enter leaves the setting as it is. A
setting named for another lane (another lane's prefix) is not asked, even when
a shared program can read it.
.PP
.B The secrets.
Each secret the chosen lanes read
.RI ( lane\-settings.list )
that is not stored yet. The check-in's Claude credential is offered as one
choice, since it needs exactly one. Yes runs
.B cti\-agent\-secrets set
.IR NAME ,
which reads the value with no echo and stores it as a systemd credential:
values never pass through this program. The list is what the programs could
read, so it can name a secret a lane needs only with an option on; store only
the ones this box uses.
.PP
Then it runs
.B fleet\-config check
and prints its result line.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Say what would be asked and written: the lanes, the empty settings, the
secrets not stored. Asks nothing and writes nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-for\-real
Ask, then write the settings and store the secrets. Root only.
.TP
.BI \-\-lanes " LANE,..."
The lanes this box runs, comma-separated, instead of asking.
.SH FILES
It writes where the box keeps its settings: on a split box the
.I /etc/cti\-agent/fleet.d/
file each setting belongs in
.RB ( file:
in
.IR settings.manifest ),
else
.IR /etc/cti\-agent/fleet.env .
A setting's line is replaced, a commented-out one is uncommented, anything
else is appended with the example's comment. Each file keeps its owner and
mode (root:ctiagent 0640) and is replaced atomically. A value with a quote, a
backslash, a $ or a newline is refused; one with a space is quoted.
.SH EXAMPLES
A new box that runs the digest, the scout feeds and the check-in only:
.PP
.nf
sudo cti\-agent\-onboard \-\-dry\-run \-\-lanes checkin,digest,scout
sudo cti\-agent\-onboard \-\-for\-real \-\-lanes checkin,digest,scout
.fi
.PP
Adding a lane later: run it again with the lane added, then enable its timer
as RUNBOOK section 9 says. Dropping one: run it again without the lane, then
\fBsudo cti\-agent\-update \-\-for\-real\fR, which turns that lane's timer off.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when done (whatever
.B fleet\-config check
says), 1 when the fleet or its settings are not installed, 2 for a usage
error or a name that is no lane.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cti\-agent\-secrets (1),
.BR cti\-agent\-settings (1),
.BR fleet\-config (1),
.BR fleet\-timers (1)
